# FlyChess project instructions

Consolidated 2026-10-06. Keep this file focused on stable instructions. Use [current status](documentation/current-status.md) for experiment outcomes and [documentation navigation](documentation/README.md) to locate designs. Historical instructions are preserved in [the journal archive](research/journal/archive/README.md).

## Current direction

The research restarted on 2026-10-30. Follow [the approved restart plan](documentation/design/research-goals/restart-plan-2026-10-30.md). Old experiments are early-development history in [the archive](archive/early-development/README.md); old curriculum goals, Stage 1 gates, token cues and the lesson planner are not current. Where a section below conflicts with the plan's input or feedback rules, the plan wins. The researcher's definition of reasoning is "being able to apply many unrelated concepts to solve an unfamiliar problem". Several agent sessions may work in this repository at once: re-read a file before editing it and leave changes you did not make alone.

## Writing style: plain words only

Write every label in plain words. Do not use codes such as P1, P2, G0, Q3 or A/B/C tags for requirements, goals, arms or results. Write "Property 1", "Property 2" and so on, or a short plain name such as "in the brain". Same rule for chat replies to the researcher. Old files keep their codes as history; new text uses words.

## Goal: the five-volume series

The research goal is to complete the five-volume series **Can a Fly Play Chess?**: 1 Methodology, 2 The Classroom, 3 Reasoning, 4 The Limits of Biology, 5 Chess. Every experiment, calculation, proof and tool exists to supply evidence for a named chapter; name the volume and chapter before proposing work. The confirmed chapters and their required content are in [the writings workspace](research/literature/writings/README.md); each volume's question and evidence boundaries are in [the series plan](documentation/design/research-goals/five-volume-series.md). Volume 3 tests the connectome with disclosed computational aids, and either outcome is evidence. Volume 4 proves bounded limits of the raw connectome model, not the living fly.

## Continuing the research

When the researcher says "let's continue our research" or similar:

1. Read [the next checkpoint](documentation/next-checkpoint.md): last checkpoint, next step, required approval, evidence map.
2. Check the artifacts against it: the newest `runs/**/manifest.json` by modification time, live `node`/`python` processes, pause flags in the selected registration under `python actions/`, and `git log -5`. Artifacts outrank documents. A manifest newer than the checkpoint file, or with a different status, means the documents are stale; a `running` status with no live process means interrupted.
3. If stale, reconstruct the outcome from that run's `manifest.json`, `summary.json` and `records.jsonl`, record it as a dated addendum in the checkpoint file, and continue from there.
4. Tell the researcher the volume and chapter, the last checkpoint, the next step, and whether it needs approval.

Done when the researcher has those four facts and the checkpoint file matches the artifacts. Launch nothing the checkpoint marks as awaiting approval.

**Checkpoint metadata.** Every evidence-bearing run directory holds a `manifest.json`, written before work starts and rewritten when it ends, with `status` (`running`, `complete`, `failed`, `interrupted`), `pid`, `argv`, code and input `pins`, start and end times, and `volume` and `chapter` naming the chapter it serves (older runs may lack the last three). Results sit beside it in `summary.json` and `records.jsonl`. This lets the latest checkpoint and its evidence be found from artifacts alone. When a run ends or the direction changes, update `documentation/next-checkpoint.md` in the same session.

## Skill routing: mandatory for relevant work

Before acting, match the request against the triggers below, including ordinary language, not just explicit skill names. Read each applicable repository `SKILL.md` and follow its workflow; mentioning a skill is not a substitute for using it. Reassess when the task changes. Combine skills when their scopes overlap. Honor explicit skill levels, modes, source limits and task boundaries. Higher-priority instructions and the research approval, experiment and evidence rules still apply.

| Skill | Load and apply when |
| --- | --- |
| [Problem Solving](.agents/skills/problem-solving/SKILL.md) | Diagnosing unexpected results, reconciling evidence, choosing among plausible approaches, unblocking work, or an explicit effort-level request. Choose the lowest sufficient level and escalate when needed. |
| [Deep Research](.agents/skills/deep-research/SKILL.md) | Verifying a claim or answering a research question from evidence, including supplied records, comparisons and literature. Follow the allowed source boundary. |
| [Ponytail](.agents/skills/ponytail/SKILL.md) | Writing, fixing, reviewing or refactoring code, or deciding on dependencies or implementation complexity. Full is the default; honor a requested level or off mode. |
| [Please No More Slop](.agents/skills/please-no-more-slop/SKILL.md) | Drafting, editing, explaining or reviewing research and technical communication, or choosing names for code, files, APIs or labels. Apply the five-year-old test to the main explanation and preserve the evidence and claim. |
| [Science Review](.agents/skills/science-review/SKILL.md) | Reviewing research quality or readiness, or when its milestone triggers apply: focused review before a decisive protocol, measure, control or test; recheck affected criteria after a substantive result or revision; full review for first evidence of the main claim, a major change to the main question or design, submission, or an overall score. Respect an explicitly narrower review. |
| [README Maestro](.agents/skills/readme-maestro/SKILL.md) | Writing or repairing READMEs, folder maps, documentation indexes, onboarding or reader navigation, including when a change affects a maintained reader route. |
| [Build Skills](.agents/skills/build-skills/SKILL.md) | Creating, fixing or reproducing a repository skill, turning a recurring workflow into a skill, or changing skill behavior or routing. Follow its testing and review protocol and report unverified checks. |
| [Delegate](.agents/skills/delegate/SKILL.md) | Asked to delegate, scope worker prompts, use parallel agents, or improve multi-agent cost or coordination; also use when deciding how to split authorized multi-agent work. The lead verifies and integrates results. |

For overlapping work, load every applicable skill. For example, a coding diagnosis can require Problem Solving, Ponytail and Please No More Slop; an evidence-based research decision can also require Deep Research and Science Review. In Heavy and deep-in-thought, incorporate Science Review into the existing review passes when its trigger applies. Delegate only when the user and runtime authorize agent use; otherwise do the applicable checks directly and disclose any material loss of independence.

Keep the work proportional. Do not trigger research, scoring, delegation or file writes solely for a copyedit, exact transformation, quotation or return-only request. Apply the relevant skill's own near misses and stopping rules; explicit requests still take precedence within the allowed scope. Open supporting references when a live decision needs them. If a required skill or dependency cannot be read, say what is unavailable, continue using the applicable repository rules, and identify any material check that remains unverified.

## Keep README navigation current

Use [README Maestro](.agents/skills/readme-maestro/SKILL.md), the README and navigation variant of Please No More Slop, when writing or reviewing folder maps, onboarding routes, or documentation indexes.

When a change adds, moves, renames, removes, or repurposes an item covered by a README map or index, update that README and affected links in the **same change**. The person changing the item owns the update. List each major folder in its nearest maintained map with a plain description. Add each new design page and experiment report to its owning index. A new file inside an already indexed experiment only needs a new index link if a reader needs it to understand the report.

Run `python .github/scripts/check-readme-navigation.py` from the repository root after staging any new navigation paths. The same check runs on GitHub. It verifies that the maintained maps cover their declared folders and that supported local README links point to tracked paths. The [check guide](.github/scripts/README.md) lists the coverage and syntax. Walk the affected reader routes to verify link wording and scientific meaning. Keep frozen files and machine-local outputs at their recorded paths.

## Python actions for recurring CLI work

Use the [Python actions guide](python%20actions/README.md) for recurring CLI work. Register exact commands with the shared runner before writing a new action. Add Python when it removes recurring launch, observation, validation or recovery work. Use a direct command for a one-off check or edit.

Save full command output and receipts in their existing logs. Return concise structured milestones, terminal state and errors to the agent. Preserve pinned commands, run identity, resource guards and recovery behavior. A failed observation does not authorize a restart. If Python is unavailable, use a compact fallback for necessary checks and state the limitation.

Use `fly.cmd` from the repository root as the short Python launcher. The [command guide](documentation/design/puzzle-learning/python-curriculum-actions.md) and [default single-step registration](python%20actions/curriculum-step.json) cover `status`, bounded `watch`, `verify`, `plan JOB`, explicit `start train|resume|audit`, `run CHECK`, `checkpoint`, `report`, and bounded `logs`. Change the step file's registered paths/pins/argv instead of rewriting shell commands. Preserve one `paths.run` identity across execution and reporting. The existing interpreter is `%USERPROFILE%\.venv-class\Scripts\python.exe`; bare `python` may be a Store alias. Keep the config paused during a research pause. A ready preview or tooling task does not authorize experiments. Read the actual CLI before configuring an unfamiliar stage; its independent audit and frozen gates remain authoritative.

## Objective and boundaries

Build a from-scratch connectome-constrained simulation that learns chess through experience and independently verified feedback on a laptop. The researcher installed an additional 8 GB RAM on 2026-10-19 and authorizes a 2 GB process budget for new, unfrozen work; preserve the registered limits of existing frozen experiments. Preserve released neuron identities, graph topology and fixed sign conventions. The teacher supplies experiences and consequences; lasting adaptation must arise through an explicitly modeled, biologically justified mechanism inside the simulation. Do not substitute pretrained chess fine-tuning, supervised criterion fitting or external search for this objective.

The user-confirmed overarching question (2026-10-10) is: **Could a simple organism achieve reasoning?** This project uses a Drosophila-connectome-constrained computational model and chess to test whether experience-dependent plasticity supports consequence-sensitive evaluation and transfer across unfamiliar chess positions and counterfactuals. Chess is a testbed for transferable reasoning; success within chess does not alone prove reasoning across domains or cognition in a living fly. The user's longer-term playing-strength target is 2000 Elo with an evaluation and style shaped by the model's own learning. That is a distinct aspiration, not a current capability or prerequisite for the first causal transfer result.

Read [feedback-learning direction](documentation/design/research-goals/feedback-learning.md) and [scientific contribution](documentation/design/research-goals/scientific-contribution.md). Puzzles are the initial learning stage, not the whole research question. Human comparison may assess a later performance claim but does not replace the causal transfer question. Both symbolic chess encoders bypass modeled eyes; natural visual perception is unimplemented.

The researcher's engineering sequence remains **working puzzle-and-feedback learning round -> latency optimization -> scaling**. The first round tests a prerequisite for the overarching reasoning/transfer question; it does not redefine it as puzzle solving. Useful chess-learning and transfer gates remain unmet. Future snapshot tracking, Stockfish-calibrated Elo evaluation and pondering are accepted designs. A calibrated 2000-Elo result is a long-term playing-strength target; Elo alone does not establish reasoning. Repeated moves, changed gains, event counts and successful external search do not establish reasoning. Neither short-run failures nor more training automatically establish universal incapacity or a cure.

When an independently audited evaluation first demonstrates transferable reasoning under the project's predefined disjoint and counterfactual gates, celebrate by saying **"EUREKA EUREKA"** repeatedly. Do not use the phrase before those gates pass.

