 ## 4. The recommendation: the replay diary
 
-**One sentence:** the diary writes down each lesson the fly actually received, and between new lessons it makes the fly re-live one randomly chosen old lesson, through its own eyes and dopamine cells, so the fly's own connections relearn the old lesson alongside the new one. It is never consulted when the fly chooses a move, and it is removed before any test.
+**One sentence:** the diary records the exact input and pulse of each lesson the fly received, and after each new lesson it makes the fly re-live one randomly chosen older lesson, so the fly's own connections practise the old lesson alongside the new one. It is never consulted when the fly chooses, and it is removed before any test.
 
-**Why this design.** Networks that learn by changing shared connections lose old learning when new material is practised in a block ([McCloskey and Cohen 1989](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0079-7421(08)60536-8)). Mixing old material back in reduces the loss ([Robins 1995](https://doi.org/10.1080/09540099550039318)). The complementary-learning-systems account goes further: a fast store of individual episodes that re-presents them to a slow learner lets the slow learner extract structure shared across many episodes, if learning is gradual and interleaved ([McClelland et al. 1995](https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-295X.102.3.419); [Kumaran et al. 2016](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tics.2016.05.004)). In machine learning, a small replay buffer filled by reservoir sampling reduces forgetting without task labels ([Chaudhry et al. 2019](https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.10486); [Rolnick et al. 2019](https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.11682)). In flies, memory consolidation after training depends on dopamine-neuron activity ([Plaçais et al. 2012](https://doi.org/10.1038/nn.3055)) and is helped by sleep ([Donlea et al. 2011](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1202249)). These fly studies motivate offline re-activation in general; they do not show that flies replay stored episodes, and this design does not claim they do.
+**Why this design.** Networks that learn by changing shared connections lose earlier learning when new material is practised in a block ([McCloskey and Cohen 1989](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0079-7421(08)60536-8)); mixing old material back in reduces the loss ([Robins 1995](https://doi.org/10.1080/09540099550039318)). The complementary-learning-systems account adds that a fast store of single episodes, re-presented gradually and interleaved, lets a slow shared learner extract structure common to many episodes ([McClelland et al. 1995](https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-295X.102.3.419); [Kumaran et al. 2016](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tics.2016.05.004)). A small replay buffer filled by reservoir sampling reduces forgetting without task labels ([Chaudhry et al. 2019](https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.10486); [Rolnick et al. 2019](https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.11682)). Fly memory consolidation after training depends on dopamine-neuron activity ([Plaçais et al. 2012](https://doi.org/10.1038/nn.3055)) and is helped by sleep ([Donlea et al. 2011](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1202249)); these motivate offline re-activation in general and are not evidence that flies replay stored episodes.
 
-The design matters for reasoning because any general rule the fly forms is then in its own connections, where the researcher's reasoning claim needs it. The diary only decides which old lessons the fly practises again.
+For reasoning, the diary's job is narrow. The transfer protocol teaches ideas one after another and then asks the fly to use them together; if learning the second idea erases the first, combining them is impossible. The diary is meant to keep earlier ideas alive while later ones are learned. Any combining is the fly's. Any general rule the fly forms is then in its own connections, where the reasoning claim needs it. The diary only decides which old lessons are practised again. Whether that produces combinable lessons or a case classifier in the weights is a test question (section 7), not an assumption.
 
-### 4.1 What enters the diary
+### 4.1 The lesson boundary and what enters the diary
 
-One entry per teaching event the fly actually received during practice, written after the online lesson and its receipt are saved.
+A lesson has four steps with separate records. The diary is written only from step 4.
 
-| Field | Content | Why it is permitted |
+1. **Choice.** The fly scores the candidates from unchanged memory and chooses (own-choice stages), or the tape names the move (stage 2). Record the unaided ranking and its hash.
+2. **Teacher.** The checker certifies only the move practised. Its certificate goes to an **audit log** that diary code cannot open.
+3. **Delivery.** One forward pass with the move's input and the teaching pulse applied together, as the current learner does (`probe-wide-learning-v3.mjs`, lines 89–91, where eye stimulation and dopamine stimulation enter the same pass), then the learning rule's update.
+4. **Receipt.** Written atomically after the update is saved.
+
+| Diary field | Content | Why permitted |
 |---|---|---|
-| `board` | The board position exactly as shown to the eyes (FEN, plus the history the eyes need, which is none today) | The fly saw it |
-| `chosen_move` | The move the fly itself chose, in UCI | The fly made it; Eyes marks it on the image |
-| `pulse` | Which dopamine group was driven (reward: PAM07 and PAM02; punishment: PPL101 and PPL103, as in the [three-board test](three-board-feedback-test-2026-11-14.md)) and the current delivered | The fly received it |
-| `lesson_index` | Order of the online lesson in the run | Bookkeeping |
-| `image_hash` | SHA-256 of the photoreceptor input rendered at the original lesson | Lets replay prove the fly sees exactly what it saw |
-| `receipt_hash` | SHA-256 of the trial receipt that delivered the online pulse | Lets the auditor rebuild the diary |
+| `stimulus` | The exact input the fly received for the move, as delivered: for chess, the Eyes v2 photoreceptor stimulation list (index, current); for stage 1, the injected Kenyon-cell code | The fly received exactly this |
+| `stimulus_hash` | SHA-256 of `stimulus` | Identity of the experience |
+| `teaching` | The external teaching input delivered: which dopamine group (reward: PAM07 and PAM02; punishment: PPL101 and PPL103) and current, or in stage 1 the valence the task's teaching function received | The fly received it |
+| `lesson_index` | Order of the lesson | Bookkeeping |
+| `receipt_hash` | SHA-256 of the receipt | Lets the auditor rebuild the diary |
+
+**Not stored in the diary:** board text (FEN), move counters and any other detail the eyes do not show; the certificate or its reasons; whether the move was good; the best move; other moves and their outcomes; the ranking and scores; engine values; validation or test inputs; lessons whose verification was unknown (no pulse was delivered). Board text and certificates stay in the audit log for analysis and auditing only.
 
-**Never written:** the checker's certificate or its reasons; whether the move was "safe"; the best move; any other move or its outcome; the fly's ranking or scores (they go to a separate diagnostic log that diary code cannot read); engine evaluations; validation or test boards; events whose verification was unknown (no pulse was delivered, so there is nothing to re-live).
+Storing the delivered input instead of the board removes the leak the peer review found: two boards that differ only in invisible move counters produce identical eye input, so they are now the same experience. It also removes any need for chess code in the diary. An entry is 13–16 KiB: measured on 83 moves from three boards, Eyes v2 drives 1,644 photoreceptors on average and at most 1,955, at 8 bytes per (index, current) pair. Even a diary holding every lesson of stage 2 (960 entries) needs about 15 MiB.
 
