-4. Correction: the reversed association ranked correctly after the reversal block with the diary.
-5. Not extra practice: the diary has a higher retention ratio than filler replay and than the same replays before the reversal, in every run.
+**Reading the result:** all pass → re-living old lessons during new learning, not extra practice, timing or re-exposure, prevents this damage on this learner. Retention passes but the reversed item fails → the diary blocks correction: a design failure. Acquisition fails → uninterpretable. **Limit:** this is the older learner, not the current revision-4 chess learner.
 
-**Reading the result:**
+### 7.4 Stage 2: learn idea A, then idea B, on a fixed chess lesson tape
 
-- All pass, and both practice controls lose an association or fall short of 0.70: re-living old lessons during new learning, not extra practice, prevents this forgetting.
-- Retention passes but correction fails: the diary buys stability by blocking correction; record as a failure of the design.
-- The interleaved-from-start arm also loses associations: the rule and representation cannot hold them together; record as a Volume 4 candidate limit and attempt a bounded proof.
-- The fly fails acquisition: uninterpretable; stop.
+**Question:** with identical lessons in every arm, does replay keep a chess idea learned first while a second idea is learned?
 
-### 7.4 Stage 2: learn idea A, then idea B, in chess
+**Ideas** (from the transfer protocol): **A = legal recapture**; **B = exchange value**. A punishes recapturable captures that lose material; B rewards recapturable captures of a piece worth at least the capturer. They are consistent under the protocol's combined rule, so interference happens only if the fly's connections mix the two features. Whether they do is unknown; the no-diary arm measures it.
 
-**Question:** does the diary keep a chess idea learned earlier while a second, conflicting-looking idea is learned?
+**Tape:** before any data, the `tape` stream draws 480 A lessons, then 480 B lessons, from the protocol's training pools under its rejection rules, each a (board, move) whose verdict is verified, with the certificate's pulse. Every arm practises this tape. Teacher-chosen practice is a disclosed clue (Read first, item 5).
 
-**Ideas:** from the transfer protocol. **A = legal recapture** (a capture that can be legally recaptured and would lose material is bad). **B = exchange value** (a capture that can always be recaptured is good when the captured piece is worth at least the capturing piece). A punishes recapturable captures; B rewards some recapturable captures. The two are consistent under the protocol's combined rule, so a learner that keeps "can be recaptured" apart from "worth more" has no conflict. A learner whose connections mix the two features will be pushed back and forth. Whether this fly is pushed is unknown; the no-diary arm measures it.
+**Unit and denominator (twin pairs).** A development twin pair is two boards where the same move has opposite verified answers; 48 pairs per idea, from 48 separate games. A pair is correct when the fly scores the move higher on the good twin; ties, within the three-board test's tolerance (10⁻⁹ + 10⁻⁷ × the largest absolute score on that board), count as wrong. Chance is 24 of 48. A second, **offset-cancelled** version scores each twin by the move's margin over the best other legal move on that board; a pair is correct when that margin is higher on the good twin. This removes any score added equally to every move on a board.
 
-**Practice:** 480 lessons of A, then 480 of B [choice, from the transfer protocol], using the protocol's training tasks, rejection rules and feedback rules unchanged. Lessons come only from the fly's own choices, with feedback from verified certificates through the dopamine cells; unknown gives nothing.
+**All-legal measure.** On the 192 development twin boards of the stage (96 per idea) that have at least one verified-good legal move (boards with none are excluded before testing and counted), take the fly's top-scoring moves within the tie tolerance. The same all-move scores give the offset-cancelled pairs. Good means a capture that is good under the protocol's combined rule; a quiet move is not good. King moves and moves the checker cannot certify are unknown. The board scores the share of tied top moves that are good. A board whose tied top set contains an unknown move is counted as unknown and left out of the denominator. If more than 5% of eligible boards are unknown, the measure is inconclusive and so is the stage.
 
-**Unit and denominator:** a development twin pair (two boards where the same move has opposite correct answers), 48 pairs per idea from 48 separate games. A pair is correct when the fly scores the move higher on the twin where it is good; ties count as wrong. Chance is 24 of 48.
+**Snapshots:** after the last A lesson (before its replay), and after the last B lesson (before its replay). Sawtooth log: during B, every 48 lessons, the A twin pairs are scored just before and just after that lesson's replay.
 
-**Required second measure (every arm, reported beside the first):** on development boards over **all legal moves**, the expected rate at which a verified safe capture is ranked first (ties split evenly). Boards where the top-ranked move's certificate is unknown are counted as unknown. If unknowns exceed 5% of boards, this measure is inconclusive.
+**Prerequisite (forgetting exists):** the no-diary arm acquires A (at least 32 of 48 after A) and then forgets: its kept share (condition 2 below) or net retention (condition 3) is below 0.70. Otherwise there is no measured forgetting to repair, and the stage says nothing about the diary.
+
+**Development boards are unseen.** Development twin boards come from games disjoint from every game on the tape, and no development `stimulus_hash` may appear on the tape or in any diary (checked before the run).
 
 **Pass (every run, diary arm, capacity one quarter):**
 
-1. **A acquired:** at least 32 of 48 A pairs after the A block. Chance of 32 or more by guessing: 1.5%.
-2. **A kept:** above-chance retention `(A after B − 24) / (A after A − 24)` at least 0.70. Reason: the project's retention requirement, applied only to the part above guessing, so a fly that forgets down to chance cannot look half-retained.
-3. **B learned:** at least 32 of 48 B pairs after the B block.
-4. **Learning not blocked:** B's pairs above chance with the diary at least 90% of B's pairs above chance without it [choice]. Reason: a memory that keeps A by stopping new learning fails the goal; 10% allows a small, stated cost.
-5. **Better than no diary, filler replay and the same replays before B** on A after B, each with bootstrap lower limit above 0.
-6. **Better than shuffled-pulse diary** on A after B, bootstrap lower limit above 0.
+1. **A acquired:** at least 32 of 48 A pairs after A. Chance of 32 or more by guessing: 1.5%.
+2. **A kept, pair by pair:** of the A pairs correct after A, at least 70% still correct after B. Reason: the project's 0.70 retention requirement, applied to the pairs actually learned, so gains on other pairs cannot hide losses.
+3. **A kept, net:** `(A after B − 24) / (A after A − 24)` at least 0.70, so guessing cannot look like retention.
+4. **B learned:** at least 32 of 48 B pairs after B.
+5. **Learning not blocked:** B pairs above chance with the diary at least 90% of those without it [choice]: a memory that keeps A by stopping new learning fails the goal; 10% allows a small, stated cost.
+6. **Offset-cancelled:** conditions 1 to 4 also hold on the offset-cancelled pairs.
+7. **Beats the practice controls:** more A pairs correct after B than no diary, filler, moved timing, no pulse and event-shuffled, each with bootstrap lower limit above 0. Because the replay controls start B in exactly the diary arm's state, those four comparisons measure keeping, not learning. If the filler arm itself lost more A than no diary (lower limit above 0), filler damaged learning; that comparison is then reported as uninformative and the others decide.
+8. **Useful choices:** all-legal measure after B above learning off, bootstrap lower limit above 0.
 
-**Reading the result:**
+**Worked example of conditions 1 to 5.** After A the diary arm has 34 of 48 A pairs right (10 above chance). After B, 25 of those 34 are still right (74%, condition 2 passes) and 6 pairs that were wrong are now right, so A after B is 31: net `(31 − 24) / (34 − 24) = 0.70`, exactly the cutoff, which passes because the rule says at least. B ends at 33 with the diary and 34 without it: `(33 − 24) / (34 − 24) = 0.90`, also exactly at its cutoff, so condition 5 passes. Had only 23 of the 34 stayed right (68%), condition 2 would fail even with the same net score.
 
-- All pass: the diary keeps chess idea A while the fly learns B, beyond extra practice and re-exposure; Property 2 with a disclosed aid, for Volume 3.
-- The no-diary arm also passes 1–3: there was no forgetting to fix at this dose; report it, and the diary claim says nothing.
-- A or B not acquired: uninterpretable for memory; it is a Volume 2 learning failure.
-- Interleaved-from-start fails: no joint setting is reachable; Volume 4 candidate.
-- Capacity curve: if the benefit appears only when the diary keeps every lesson, the design reduces to "store everything" and does not scale; record as a failure under pressure.
+**Order is not counterbalanced.** Every run learns A first. A pass says the first-taught idea A survived B; it does not show that any first-taught idea survives.
 
-### 7.5 Stage 3: transfer and combination
+**Reading the result:** all pass → replay keeps chess idea A while B is learned, beyond extra practice, timing, re-exposure and wrong verdicts: assisted retention, Volume 3. Large sawtooth with a low-point failure → refresh, not joint storage. Mixed order succeeds but the diary fails → a joint setting is reachable and this replay schedule does not find it. Mixed order fails → this schedule failed; a Volume 4 limit needs a separate bounded proof or exact search over the permitted connection strengths, as in the revision-4 capacity checks. Capacity curve: a benefit only when the diary keeps every lesson means the design does not work under pressure.
 
